[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24811889-2c2a-5489-87b6-7ec9fcc9ec5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339445} {:answer/id #uuid "49559a38-7682-51c6-961d-af8f6d4b9c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339786} {:answer/id #uuid "8e550649-e7ec-521f-b83e-ccfcdfe9298e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340407} {:answer/id #uuid "b182cd8c-78d4-5443-bfc8-d64291dce17e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340743} {:answer/id #uuid "b2f9dc50-f6a9-5e82-a931-a20f17095169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b182cd8c-78d4-5443-bfc8-d64291dce17e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340743} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cc71ef4-f030-5214-97b4-d07a1eb70ea5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe7192f0-0469-5c77-92a1-060194834d30" :question/math-academy-id "q-73482" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{x+1}}?$   \n\n- $[-1,0]$\n- $[1,10]$\n- $(-1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,0]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because it is not defined at $x=-1.$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,10]$ is  closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,10].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval  $(-1,0)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a68717c-3c5f-58a5-9174-e8f45f3e1ef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339216} {:answer/id #uuid "336f07da-9df2-582d-80ca-e44d8eb292b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339585} {:answer/id #uuid "43af0409-9e51-5d17-a8fb-98b37bd00bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339722} {:answer/id #uuid "72581646-406e-5e01-9bba-7ad77de94142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340144} {:answer/id #uuid "8e33f721-3fb7-5e44-8644-c40ab2c07185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "336f07da-9df2-582d-80ca-e44d8eb292b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339585} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a35e850-44d5-5740-b0b8-70909d0b180d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5aba1141-c0a4-54c1-bd64-30eb78786676" :question/math-academy-id "q-73484" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}?$    \n\n- $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$\n- $[\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n- $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$  is closed, but the function is not defined on a part of the interval because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$    is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ is  closed, and the function is continuous on $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51327576-3dc9-528f-9e13-d755abf55471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339872} {:answer/id #uuid "7b3f93d2-0ebe-5934-8c91-fce10e0d1450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340232} {:answer/id #uuid "90e67802-13a1-5947-86e3-d0c565f61870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340428} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6c8074-427b-5dfe-9ad1-218b184fe226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340690} {:answer/id #uuid "ef2a0a0c-7790-530c-89f1-dbee9d7d9e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90e67802-13a1-5947-86e3-d0c565f61870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340428} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b5cc245-bbea-5aaf-8a56-ee9acee3fb23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e05c6d3d-29e1-53ab-9c70-1ad2b27199a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-73486" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{x})?$   \n\n- $[\\frac{1}{2},1]$\n- $[0,\\frac{1}{4})$\n- $[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[\\frac{1}{2},1]$   is closed, and the function is continuous on $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$   Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,\\frac{1}{4})$  is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,1]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because it is not defined at $x=0.$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341988 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a506d32-c549-5247-82e3-f237fa06872d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339215} {:answer/id #uuid "22362652-f0bc-5e3c-afa7-25ce82d78661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339421} {:answer/id #uuid "327c0e31-b1ed-5548-81bc-18ff4bca8bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339573} {:answer/id #uuid "87824f92-9edf-52ca-9f8c-b3eeb64b58cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340341} {:answer/id #uuid "a19759de-fa06-5d86-925a-320a7299775b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a19759de-fa06-5d86-925a-320a7299775b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340594} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a8dae27-98ed-5b9c-b45d-d7007b99d819" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96d14ec6-ae06-5360-8ddd-d401dbb48508" :question/math-academy-id "q-73487" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{x}{{2}^{x}-1}?$    \n\n- $[0,1]$\n- $[0,1)$\n- $[-2,-1]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[0,1]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because it is not defined at $x=0$ (it makes zero the denominator).   Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval  $[0,1)$   is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-2,-1]$    is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-2,-1].$   Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27cfa7a4-d6c1-5351-99b2-6635044d85f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339474} {:answer/id #uuid "65459a76-323b-5a88-8a2f-2e108e43ea11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340024} {:answer/id #uuid "88971863-2310-5e3f-ac5d-6e4e1adfb7df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340348} {:answer/id #uuid "91912873-03a1-524d-9ad0-7c617923af84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340433} {:answer/id #uuid "be62f3bd-2eeb-5b57-889c-ca65dbe099b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91912873-03a1-524d-9ad0-7c617923af84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340433} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcb8b636-417c-59ba-b9cc-599917ded547" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7b9b884e-eb8a-59c7-aa31-49f0025fb47f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73495" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ -1, & x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[-1,1]$\n- $[\\frac{1}{2},1]$\n- $[-1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[-1,1].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}({2}^{-x})={2}^{0}=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$ In this interval, $f(x)={2}^{-x}$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $[-1,0)$, is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ceb0ce8-da1c-5b99-9182-d772be7cec72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339520} {:answer/id #uuid "7896c996-e406-5136-a44c-25645e86bbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340203} {:answer/id #uuid "9a19840a-be8c-5757-bad3-4daa8391bbb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340517} {:answer/id #uuid "a718fb5b-1dd8-5664-a8f8-13023ada0bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340646} {:answer/id #uuid "f499afb4-f0c7-5505-912f-71dee8dd2498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a19840a-be8c-5757-bad3-4daa8391bbb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340517} :answer-field/id #uuid "141d901b-70e2-5398-8913-6c50c5c4a073" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d1eaad6e-884d-57bd-a4ae-a690d3d6abe9" :question/math-academy-id "q-73499" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1), & x>1 \\\\ x-1, & x\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[0,2]$\n- $[-1,0]$\n- $[-1,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[0,2].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[-1,0].$ In this interval, $f(x)=x-1$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,0].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, in the interval $[-1,2]$, we also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,2]$ and therefore  the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "235e2186-c516-5a12-99e1-855472aad9f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339433} {:answer/id #uuid "7e1ad79a-9054-5adb-8e27-82c74fb0eeee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340265} {:answer/id #uuid "d65d9629-6375-5d3b-9bf7-be4b8ce2e02b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341027} {:answer/id #uuid "de97e50a-3ac0-5909-8788-42a53cdaa913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341105} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a1b048-a2c7-5d2d-99eb-e4c76eea7395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e1ad79a-9054-5adb-8e27-82c74fb0eeee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340265} :answer-field/id #uuid "3883c8f8-56cb-5ac2-9602-b5a9a360bba9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a761269-f6ea-50eb-8e45-58983c3d9692" :question/math-academy-id "q-73503" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ \\,\\,2, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[-1,1]$\n- $(0,3]$\n- $[-2,-1]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- First, let's consider $[-1,1].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}=\\frac{2-0}{{2}^{0}+1}=1\\ne f(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[-2,-1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[-2,-1].$  Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[-2,-1],$  and the extreme value theorem is also applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f8ca629-0927-538d-ab6c-ffef86ed4c3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339401} {:answer/id #uuid "3cbeae54-1a8e-537b-bcc8-b644943612ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339669} {:answer/id #uuid "4680f954-f4f8-5258-ae55-a7412a19432c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339754} {:answer/id #uuid "bfda1598-8520-5bca-930a-7ba3397c4c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340839} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb6ae8f-aab7-5bdc-8f7c-dc14885c5a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cbeae54-1a8e-537b-bcc8-b644943612ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339669} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c36c391-b98e-5750-8019-3f694dde0b5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dcdd6cca-dc15-5e0c-a55e-4d73a59866ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-73504" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2{x}^{3}-1, & x<1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡(x-1), & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[-1,2]$\n- $(1,3]$\n- $[2,\\pi ]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[-1,2].$  We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(2{x}^{3}-1)=2(1)^{3}-1=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}{sin}^{2}⁡(x-1)={sin}^{2}⁡(1-1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(1,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[2,\\pi ].$ In this interval, $f(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(x-1)$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,\\pi ].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085a372e-3d44-5218-8ffc-7245e73f20e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339197} {:answer/id #uuid "27bfa14d-cc0f-5a6b-9127-444c2c3cbe94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339472} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff1f004-8a24-53f6-8512-8daed863b9af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339982} {:answer/id #uuid "807a16d0-c898-5c5a-aa54-af34b1d71126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340280} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef8375b-1f86-5526-a7e2-474095047082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "807a16d0-c898-5c5a-aa54-af34b1d71126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340280} :answer-field/id #uuid "1167d122-71e9-5a13-8edb-8b2e825eb1e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8abc4b9f-caf8-5c94-91da-62ad0ae61b3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-73531" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{x-1}-1}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ \\,\\,1, & x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[-2,2]$\n- $[-3,1)$\n- $[1.5,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- Let first consider the interval  $[-2,2].$ This is a  closed interval, but we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{2}^{x-1}-1}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-3,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\n- Finally consider  $[1.5,2].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=1,$ which is not in the interval  $[1.5,2].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[1.5,2]$, and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f079a8-3eef-5e0c-b508-56b0a28e810a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339187} {:answer/id #uuid "33e2246e-0cb8-5b3d-a663-6ded80537d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339590} {:answer/id #uuid "36e9f6d3-ade4-54f2-83b8-98b440f7e2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339621} {:answer/id #uuid "9be8a799-d92f-523e-80f4-9f217caeda37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340530} {:answer/id #uuid "a94a7551-868d-5a9d-8d58-61cf61d367d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9be8a799-d92f-523e-80f4-9f217caeda37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340530} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c233766-89ba-51e9-91fa-b4ab683f8678" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "451e02c3-10e2-5101-a83f-538a8be6b03d" :question/math-academy-id "q-73549" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the ellipses $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1?$\n\n- It is a type I region\n- It is a type II region\n- It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/62/627e7e102a0f16cb0c4946e42b48ad122299d729c5bc419293e258a3f2e1701e.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=0.25$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](images/d7/d7aa48c887d6fb1c3d0420867b7e74010fa93e16d7e24317e2cf214764e56113.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=0.5$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](images/ec/ec632fa5f86d8d9b34b9ccf661a3219e965f289c28bf69d7d80eeeb46093e7ae.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d0942ba-bce4-5a73-b8f5-3a15b4943c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339521} {:answer/id #uuid "31ae29f6-67fa-5cce-aa91-ab942cc5831e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339564} {:answer/id #uuid "3b302601-cdd3-56f8-a749-be1e8bfc6735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339656} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc6db8a-3711-5dad-924b-ebe119b6531b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340978} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e50ccd-c0bc-5e24-9964-dbcbc4fe9a4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d0942ba-bce4-5a73-b8f5-3a15b4943c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339521} :answer-field/id #uuid "9be10fcd-f13d-57ed-917c-3ac7e1f3939e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6905816-c0d6-59f0-9ff1-518732f97672" :question/math-academy-id "q-73562" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=0$ in the usual way. Given that the system has a unique zero solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $A$ is not invertible\n- The system $Ax=b$ has no solution for some $2\\times 1$ column vectors $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c953414-3739-5046-8dec-725e4aebce23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339239} {:answer/id #uuid "4f00f0c1-1128-5dc5-9ada-1163d47376f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339847} {:answer/id #uuid "59c27354-029f-5161-8275-5f32032b4d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339944} {:answer/id #uuid "676720c2-ddc1-5eb9-bc37-6bba3e1a79a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340045} {:answer/id #uuid "93ad7075-67b9-5402-8c92-fa920b5b9d4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93ad7075-67b9-5402-8c92-fa920b5b9d4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340454} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae784b4f-476e-5ef2-b41b-614da6bae2c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12c8cd48-ec61-5529-9409-02950b9c8c07" :question/math-academy-id "q-73563" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=0$ in the usual way. Given that the system has a unique zero solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- The system $Ax=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has exactly one solution\n- $A$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements II  and III are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2121f091-3a66-5690-a210-70cac8d8b961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339414} {:answer/id #uuid "641ee5b3-8fa1-5cee-afcc-4789bc026277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340016} {:answer/id #uuid "ae19279b-2df0-59ff-8f89-720ecde46013" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340713} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c9164f-2c54-5305-863c-aac35ca8a133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341173} {:answer/id #uuid "e8cc863f-3945-59c2-9bb7-328ec9d3b8ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7c9164f-2c54-5305-863c-aac35ca8a133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341173} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b0541d7-b06d-5667-8592-a8a8781d2700" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b4e1216-e41e-5317-bf20-2acb3db54b81" :question/math-academy-id "q-73565" :question/problem "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- The lines $2x+4y=0$ and $3x+6y=0$ intersect at exactly one point\n- ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 6-4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is false, all the other parts of the theorem are false too. Therefore, according to the theorem: \n\n- Statement II is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "046da6c0-c585-5c82-a45f-c7c3afa67856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339158} {:answer/id #uuid "22874900-86d0-5ea5-9f8a-e42fae76e5fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339425} {:answer/id #uuid "74e58a36-845a-5c3a-8a9d-ef11d9806c7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340174} {:answer/id #uuid "85e8002d-e487-5016-84c1-5eeec3a16860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340330} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2764b9-f3c6-5f02-8bc2-30cd14016830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee2764b9-f3c6-5f02-8bc2-30cd14016830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341229} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfad9c66-30b6-5b34-8a1b-2bc285b67454" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "946ddb30-e9a3-52e2-ad46-5aa2c1f15eef" :question/math-academy-id "q-73567" :question/problem "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 3.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- The lines $2x+2y=2$ and $3x+ky=0$ intersect at infinitely many points\n- $Ax=0$ has the unique solution $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot k-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $2k-6\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 3.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- Statement III is true by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statement III is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342018 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f4a89ce-e05f-5059-8a98-0662c58dca06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339698} {:answer/id #uuid "676c1f80-92a5-555a-90c0-724cd922d308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340046} {:answer/id #uuid "d829cd88-1a41-56d8-8a9f-50e044938531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341046} {:answer/id #uuid "f2321685-a5b3-5098-a3f0-26eb25584ade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341261} {:answer/id #uuid "f28ff253-4f5c-582e-beba-3b0d6c5b3849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "676c1f80-92a5-555a-90c0-724cd922d308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340046} :answer-field/id #uuid "65e0c694-9cf8-58c5-9e10-b36e1dd82270" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb595552-3588-5923-aa25-072e92fab82f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73570" :question/problem "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 8.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- The system $Ax=b$ has infinitely many solutions\n- The lines $x+2y=2$ and $4x+ky=0$ don't intersect" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =k-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $k-8\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 8.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false.  According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement I is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124477 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "488c9762-554b-5ea6-b414-ce6cbde9bbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pivot Columns of a Matrix in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47637797-0ccd-5e65-82c6-35111ab66cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339759} {:answer/id #uuid "609bf16a-a52c-525d-b8ef-4c25aa5e1cd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339989} {:answer/id #uuid "6ded5be2-0cd6-5996-9b1a-87a748b79f1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $2$nd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340101} {:answer/id #uuid "77131162-9719-515d-8206-e4551f77375f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340187} {:answer/id #uuid "811562bb-1ccc-5de3-be33-bb0c9aeeaba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47637797-0ccd-5e65-82c6-35111ab66cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339759} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e0cc6cb-f9e7-5623-a1df-aa514c998025" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b24da91d-4cea-58f0-be32-4afbd9e924b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-73607" :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $B$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $B?$\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $B$ has $2$ pivots in the $1$st and $3$rd columns:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124477 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "488c9762-554b-5ea6-b414-ce6cbde9bbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pivot Columns of a Matrix in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19d60b30-aee1-5cae-9a82-1c26c331bb0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339355} {:answer/id #uuid "33032f4e-87ae-5c56-85f5-b993be0243fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339579} {:answer/id #uuid "61558a0c-9a4d-5cad-8be7-007004556310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339997} {:answer/id #uuid "dcaad8a4-ad75-5d03-a964-05d69c65df9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $2$nd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341088} {:answer/id #uuid "ebff5fc5-2ff1-5c17-9394-26c4e0da59ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33032f4e-87ae-5c56-85f5-b993be0243fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339579} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e4de38e-8877-52a8-b029-a002e4191a20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5c6439d-a09e-5872-bb6c-985034969e09" :question/math-academy-id "q-73609" :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $M$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $M?$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $M$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124477 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "488c9762-554b-5ea6-b414-ce6cbde9bbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pivot Columns of a Matrix in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f73df29-a20e-5ab6-befc-2452f29cbb4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339271} {:answer/id #uuid "2e981f00-0b4c-54f5-b56f-5944c17425a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339539} {:answer/id #uuid "3e778f3f-3d93-5621-b021-980061e030a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/t