th-academy-id "q-73618" :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $P$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 & -1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $P$ has $3$ pivots in the $2$nd, $4$th and $5$th columns:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 & -1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd column."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124478 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5f0d2e9-1cf6-5fe4-85cc-f401382f39b2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Non-Pivot Columns of a Matrix in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d67e28c-a256-5a1d-9e04-f477d00894a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339387} {:answer/id #uuid "260f4b80-53d9-5f26-b97b-2b2e97e88497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd, $4$th, and $5$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339456} {:answer/id #uuid "2def53e1-c883-56b0-9c46-4ebe4d3dbc36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd, $3$rd, and $5$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339534} {:answer/id #uuid "6092a204-0ea0-56e8-9213-ebd6bcdfacc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339988} {:answer/id #uuid "d76a0d0f-e606-54f1-91c8-3c6c587ad587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd and $5$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d76a0d0f-e606-54f1-91c8-3c6c587ad587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd and $5$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341037} :answer-field/id #uuid "72bbb48e-fe4e-545a-9b68-754256645b24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5f1860a-14f7-5dde-8785-3643838d3e4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73620" :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $A$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $A$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $2$nd and $4$th columns:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot columns are the $3$rd and $5$th columns."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124480 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1a557d6-52f3-5df3-8088-5b875296e72e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pivot Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c1ee36a-4db5-53af-aad2-6e0f7d75bf06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339233} {:answer/id #uuid "31c1489a-e505-5968-a0fe-1c13d9448994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339565} {:answer/id #uuid "8af06da7-434a-5cf5-a301-e37cb45b27db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd, and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340371} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c4ebf5-c3f7-51fd-acf7-a9712d8515e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340632} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d8599e-872a-5853-ab9c-14124febdd25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, $3$rd and $4$th" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5c4ebf5-c3f7-51fd-acf7-a9712d8515e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340632} :answer-field/id #uuid "72bd5908-df4a-5071-9c06-4a211c439573" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e012b726-5b5c-59b3-9af4-421c826c1005" :question/math-academy-id "q-73636" :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $B,$ given by\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $B$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $B$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342046 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124480 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1a557d6-52f3-5df3-8088-5b875296e72e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pivot Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9158a1e-ec92-5a85-91c0-20a1ac46a48d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340666} {:answer/id #uuid "b2728011-3f39-5c0e-80c4-f7db4bfbdeeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd, $3$rd, and $6$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340750} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9e67c6-6e81-5e3b-be7d-db793cd3b0e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd, $3$rd, $5$th, and $6$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340820} {:answer/id #uuid "d96d7546-90a3-580f-9f5a-249f3f3451e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, $3$rd, $5$th, and $6$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341057} {:answer/id #uuid "da721718-1f5a-5297-b2fc-1ff9bfdec092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $6$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd9e67c6-6e81-5e3b-be7d-db793cd3b0e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd, $3$rd, $5$th, and $6$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340820} :answer-field/id #uuid "31d2b7f2-9d9e-5f2d-9a1c-eeaf7743cd2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b6c62c9-235a-5e15-9835-d3716c4119bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-73639" :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 0 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $P$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 0 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $P$ has $4$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $2$nd, $3$rd, $5$th and $6$th columns."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41c2d289-8df5-551b-9501-3a762c67386f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0648e464-0804-543e-b3ba-eeb483de0ebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339181} {:answer/id #uuid "232d6fe1-daae-5d86-8404-b4de8febd878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339429} {:answer/id #uuid "4fac726b-5f45-5ab7-9a57-072105f86469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339853} {:answer/id #uuid "6706ff39-690d-5941-a362-fdddc4dab18e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340036} {:answer/id #uuid "85e79180-e571-5fc8-bd94-7c43c76f7b08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85e79180-e571-5fc8-bd94-7c43c76f7b08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340329} :answer-field/id #uuid "992a8384-a086-5092-b201-93a52d9199f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec2dfaf1-eafb-5d88-8d0f-be9d2909829b" :question/math-academy-id "q-74049" :question/problem "Suppose $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $a\\cdot a<0$\n- $b\\cdot a=a\\cdot a$\n- $b\\cdot a<0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $a\\ne 0$, we must have $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\n- Statement II is false while statement III is true. Simplifying the expression $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+b\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ b\\cdot a & =-a\\cdot a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0$, we obtain that $-a\\cdot a<0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41c2d289-8df5-551b-9501-3a762c67386f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cd32271-dc5a-5ef5-9de6-221af52fa72d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339519} {:answer/id #uuid "cb4de396-9b2c-5b13-aef6-a5e899e56c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340929} {:answer/id #uuid "ed3be0c9-bb05-529a-b75a-d2226e0c878c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341221} {:answer/id #uuid "f00bbc30-d42f-57b1-a3ca-0b3e8b0038ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341243} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d2a831-2f45-5411-9d1a-f36b6ba2ec81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cd32271-dc5a-5ef5-9de6-221af52fa72d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339519} :answer-field/id #uuid "60dfcf4a-5fb8-5dc8-acbc-e1191345ca9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "576bb373-56a3-5da0-ab43-2d02b000f274" :question/math-academy-id "q-74051" :question/problem "Suppose $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $a\\cdot a=0$\n- $a\\cdot b>0$\n- $a\\cdot b<0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $a\\ne 0$, we must have $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\n- Statement II is false while statement III is true. Simplifying the expression $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+b\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot b & =-a\\cdot a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0$, we obtain that $-a\\cdot a<0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db4180e-9e56-5615-a261-a06b057afbc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339254} {:answer/id #uuid "44382309-a210-5068-b421-83edd371f540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339728} {:answer/id #uuid "705bf110-f45f-5748-8977-a6e4eaab55d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340124} {:answer/id #uuid "a661de8b-e986-5edb-ae2a-54d4fbff884d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340638} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1d59d6-b1c0-5ab1-b672-2772922e372b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "705bf110-f45f-5748-8977-a6e4eaab55d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340124} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4646991-5d1f-5339-9e5a-2aa1bae21d22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5e93e600-4a01-5ada-842d-9b175b57ee56" :question/math-academy-id "q-74217" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $A={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:1-x-y=0}$\n- $B={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- $C={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:2x+3y-z=0}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $1-x-y=0$ since $1-0-0\\ne 0.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace. Indeed, the vector $u=⟨1,0,0⟩$ is in $B$, but $2u=⟨2,0,0⟩$ is not in $B$, since ${2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}≰1.$ So, $B$ is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $2x+3y-z=0$ since $2\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43ab7da0-2f07-50d1-831b-c157d66b1de8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339721} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffb62c9-06bc-54ed-a237-28e9635fc6fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340121} {:answer/id #uuid "72703321-1679-5b08-aab6-32e985e25b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340146} {:answer/id #uuid "74517a98-ee70-5879-af22-ee6f1cbffcda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340165} {:answer/id #uuid "ef265337-e4c5-5117-bd72-923b375019cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74517a98-ee70-5879-af22-ee6f1cbffcda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340165} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa75a06c-11fb-5ef6-a1f6-3f25d8c6ebf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5037faf6-6f8a-5ea2-800f-2c136a3bb59e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74218" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $A={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1}$\n- $B={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:xyz=0}$\n- $C={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:y=-1}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ since ${0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace because it is not closed under vector addition. Indeed, both $a=⟨1,0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,1,1⟩$ belong to $B$ but \n\n$$\na+b=⟨1,0,1⟩+⟨0,1,1⟩=⟨1,1,2⟩\n$$\n\n \ndoes not satisfy the condition $xyz=0$ since $1\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\ne 0.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $y=-1$ since $0\\ne -1.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3171ea47-31ac-55ef-8e65-7c8cc13d17db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339563} {:answer/id #uuid "4780f3c7-7445-5e20-b679-8d09700f5b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339761} {:answer/id #uuid "69308677-f0aa-523b-9be3-7371c6dc3d65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340063} {:answer/id #uuid "7458caf1-0896-52f4-b93d-16c6705c4441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340166} {:answer/id #uuid "962e504c-ea04-57e3-acb7-34e1af23490d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69308677-f0aa-523b-9be3-7371c6dc3d65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340063} :answer-field/id #uuid "df4036f4-0b20-53ba-89f9-e6eee6e466b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb81e4a8-c5a7-59da-b168-b278b747b24a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74255" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $A={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}<\\frac{1}{2}}$\n- $B={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:5x-y-z=0}$\n- $C={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:5-x-z=0}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace. Indeed, the vector $u=⟨\\frac{1}{4},0,0⟩$ is in $A$, since\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}<\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nbut, $4u=⟨1,0,0⟩$ is not in $B$, since \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}>\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $5x-y-z=0$ since $5\\cdot 0-0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $5-x-z=0$ since $5-0-0\\ne 0.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342194 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e69a4aee-6e92-598b-99b5-1d830b6dea54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Correct Statements About a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dbca51b-7f54-5a7a-ba5f-3d88cb4bc95a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339529} {:answer/id #uuid "55b791d8-4371-5bf9-bd1e-a54e720de779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339913} {:answer/id #uuid "7b095170-c16f-5974-ab98-b3690a252ff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340228} {:answer/id #uuid "caef5ea6-785c-5dcb-bc6b-8144853d5c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340925} {:answer/id #uuid "e368e4ef-99eb-518e-bdb0-6a3a39964aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e368e4ef-99eb-518e-bdb0-6a3a39964aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341141} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0391ab3-a597-51aa-a3d8-ce337f570b0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e68d4b2f-c3b6-584c-8430-662a571e8316" :question/math-academy-id "q-74318" :question/problem "Consider the matrix \n$C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $C$ is upper triangular\n- $C$ is lower triangular\n- ${C}^{T}$ is upper triangular" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. All the entries below the main diagonal of $C$ are zeros:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, since $C$ has non-zero entries above the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false, since ${C}^{T}$ has non-zero entries below the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e69a4aee-6e92-598b-99b5-1d830b6dea54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Correct Statements About a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34777553-5006-5f5c-9727-020effdb1863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339594} {:answer/id #uuid "3dc969e4-1f40-5c0a-b425-d47fcc951c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339680} {:answer/id #uuid "961268ff-d9a5-5b91-83a8-57bc579b7229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340482} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ed66f7-ff3c-5b64-aded-bf5edcca033c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340854} {:answer/id #uuid "f144d22f-284c-5b5a-bb1b-1dd2f910f9dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f144d22f-284c-5b5a-bb1b-1dd2f910f9dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341254} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5048421-85b3-572d-8328-77b24b9d125e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10915988-162b-5dec-9de2-5241d5fc3202" :question/math-academy-id "q-74319" :question/problem "Consider the matrix \n$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D$ is upper triangular\n- $D$ is lower triangular\n- ${D}^{T}$ is upper triangular" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, since $D$ has non-zero entries below the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. All the entries above the main diagonal of $D$ are zeros:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. All the entries below the main diagonal of ${D}^{T}$ are zeros:\n\n$$\n{D}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115299 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31782fc1-be3a-570b-83a4-6a253ceacbe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342654} {:answer/id #uuid "49cbd863-e93c-5db8-869b-809f678b3040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342845} {:answer/id #uuid "99a2377c-7d5c-50a2-9c01-dffd06f0e462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343588} {:answer/id #uuid "b57d65ad-ebfa-5f7c-b1f8-4c03118d3947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343829} {:answer/id #uuid "b7aa2865-0c50-5f22-80eb-6161f09775ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99a2377c-7d5c-50a2-9c01-dffd06f0e462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343588} :answer-field/id #uuid "4da73cdd-da21-5939-a610-458af60d2aec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "edc80b1b-d469-5071-b24d-3c90bb47fce6" :question/math-academy-id "q-74635" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & -2x+4y+3z=0 \\\\  & x+3y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system has no solution\n- The system is consistent independent\n- The system is consistent dependent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first try to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & \\frac{3}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means that  we can find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement III is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115299 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01308e53-b92a-5d41-8e41-3edcada2f882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342256} {:answer/id #uuid "1c251f13-a18e-56fb-b700-41d13ecaa3bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342477} {:answer/id #uuid "b0020fb9-0863-546c-95a8-236e93a1dd5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343785} {:answer/id #uuid "c309108b-fd3a-5760-8a95-009d5ec4b565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343955} {:answer/id #uuid "d75038c3-3bd3-5a22-8af1-6c84cbbc8269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c251f13-a18e-56fb-b700-41d13ecaa3bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342477} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1cccc34-d274-5062-9c39-f9e811dad04c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0089d19-a4b5-5ec4-beaa-917a294458ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-74637" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x+3y=-1 \\\\  & x-2y=1 \\\\  & -x-5y=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system has no solution\n- The system is consistent independent\n- The system is consistent dependent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first try to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ are solutions to the third equation $0x+0y=0$, we can remove this equation from the system. So, the corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=1 \\\\ 7y=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=-\\frac{3}{7}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-2(-\\frac{3}{7})=1\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\n \nSo, we conclude that the system is consistent independent. \n\nAs a result, only statement II is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74d47c5c-d9b2-5729-b276-f386649b4836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343248} {:answer/id #uuid "78689969-fa12-5d79-b86b-75a95aac6c30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343278} {:answer/id #uuid "93f165e9-4fa8-5327-af87-0aecc43a6729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343532} {:answer/id #uuid "b3d03412-f273-52d2-a7f8-85526134a1c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343815} {:answer/id #uuid "f7884f74-457b-566e-ac8e-ae3ebcb6b395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93f165e9-4fa8-5327-af87-0aecc43a6729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343532} :answer-field/id #uuid "196a3494-8261-5fa3-924a-5e54ae02aac6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5fe00a2-ab56-5281-b3c2-6fe8dad2417a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74964" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$\n- The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$\n- The limit exists and is equal to $2$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}-0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x(2x-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{2x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot 0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is also true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b9ca939-c7a6-5aeb-98c2-585d48ff8c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342604} {:answer/id #uuid "5716cd24-5a2c-5544-92f6-203e6a676437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342963} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2c0063-1ae4-55ca-87d1-b65a1e62305f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343173} {:answer/id #uuid "731035c9-6220-52ed-886c-05ae731b34ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343235} {:answer/id #uuid "cf56f8d6-4634-5189-80bd-37357cd208d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b9ca939-c7a6-5aeb-98c2-585d48ff8c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342604} :answer-field/id #uuid "51ec1b91-00af-5481-ab92-af724f5e618b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89a636cd-a216-5d73-bd3f-7f9cbe4badf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-75116" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $C?$\n\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $C,$ we write $C=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nC=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form  because  the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains a number not equal to $1.$\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ada63ec-fa1d-5ff7-b669-92737dcdbd74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342330} {:answer/id #uuid "1752c8df-b894-5390-b173-aa620c63a4ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342443} {:answer/id #uuid "409dfa57-961d-5f83-aa15-10d15babbe22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342771} {:answer/id #uuid "467b7351-7ecb-539b-ba6e-6ea1995a8925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342818} {:answer/id #uuid "98410b31-6e91-581a-a43b-5311f6c98db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "467b7351-7ecb-539b-ba6e-6ea1995a8925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342818} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c138955-72ce-5ce7-a05a-28705f2a9e68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0305a43d-bf81-5ecb-8a77-49a30ade0147" :question/math-academy-id "q-75117" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:  \n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as it has an entry not equal to $1$  on the main diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal. \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "454ea782-196e-50f9-b1e2-00d2d1bb0539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342808} {:answer/id #uuid "4f08aad3-17c0-5aea-a1e3-8bc3cab0aea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342888} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2b2691-bd26-5b75-bf4a-0ab5f6a47c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342891} {:answer/id #uuid "567c9971-93d6-5157-ae62-fc0890790260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342958} {:answer/id #uuid "9b54534f-b827-5c6c-a649-e71657a1ca05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "567c9971-93d6-5157-ae62-fc0890790260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342958} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc036018-08d5-5220-9a04-6989a4c32339" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34143660-ecfa-5585-b65e-76b215171706" :question/math-academy-id "q-75123" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $C?$\n\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $C,$ we write $C=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nC=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry above the main diagonal. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as it has an entry not equal to $1$ on the main diagonal.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f2376a1-da00-5911-b146-c5236752448e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342890} {:answer/id #uuid "58038b76-6478-52e3-88f8-675c4aa414d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342974} {:answer/id #uuid "59103f60-0dff-5bf6-af9c-6267f890a47e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342987} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea948d9-d1d3-5312-9f2b-e7b8089e8034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343638} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7d0947-9c9d-5773-b575-44075078af0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ea948d9-d1d3-5312-9f2b-e7b8089e8034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343638} :answer-field/id #uuid "f466101d-ee92-54d3-b4e2-a9cca8e95913" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21485ec9-747a-5129-ba2c-794c570827bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-75496" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dad3104-8a81-5836-a5a9-4e944d9ea89c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342747} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa425cd-c3c4-550e-9bf6-9b7007b60940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343145} {:answer/id #uuid "7230a1e9-8e89-5267-aa7f-a81a5c6cccc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343226} {:answer/id #uuid "9b1e7dd8-43cf-517f-b457-c549b0d0e69e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343606} {:answer/id #uuid "ae816524-bdcc-57d3-b1f0-57315f7a80b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7230a1e9-8e89-5267-aa7f-a81a5c6cccc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343226} :answer-field